          "resourceKind": "html",
          "contentBody": "Basic Page\nWriting a Lab Report\nThe next task is the writing of the lab report.  Preparing the report requires you to organize, into one large document, the information you have already collected.  If a researcher wants to publish his or her report in a magazine or journal, an abstract must be included at the start of the report.  An abstract is a short summary of the experiment and includes the purpose and/or hypothesis, procedure, and results.  A student who intends to enter an experiment in a science fair must include an abstract with the report.Lab reports also include an acknowledgement section (if applicable) and a reference section (bibliography).  Each of these sections is on a separate page at the end of the report.   For help creating a bibliography, you may use an online tool such as Easybib.com or you can follow the format used on the references page found under the Course Information section of this course.\nLab Report Example\nClick on the link below for an example of a lab report.  Please disregard the fact that the report is for a traditional science experiment and that it uses dependent and independent variables instead of manipulated and responding variables.\nWhat Battery is Better? Lab Report\nYou will need to reference this lab report in the future.\nabstract:  a summary of a report or experiment; the summary placed at the beginning of a report\n(this link opens in a new window/tab)",
