          "sourceHref": "сontent/i85443228-7fc3-43b1-be21-687ee68dcd1c/Uses of Psychology.html",
          "resourceKind": "html",
          "contentBody": "Basic Page\nUses of Psychology Today\n\" What's the usual tip?\" a man growled at the college boy who delivered his pizza.\" Well,\" the student replied, \" this is my first delivery, but the other guys said that if I got a quarter out of you, I'd be doing great.\"\" That so?\" grunted the man. \" In that case, here's five dollars.\"\" Thanks,\" the student said, \" I'll put it in my college fund.\"\" By the way, what are you studying?\"\" Applied psychology.\"\nApplied Psychology\nAll joking aside, the use of knowledge of how people behave is called applied psychology.  Teachers, advertisers, friends, and family use applied psychology frequently.   Have you ever wondered why milk is placed farthest from the entrance at grocery stores?  Do you know why phone numbers are \"chunked\" into three and four digit sections?  Do you know why some children do not get dessert until they eat their vegetables?The answers to these questions (and many more) relate to psychology.  Grocery store owners want customers to buy more than \"just milk\" so they place milk farthest away from the door in the hope that customers place some other product into their shopping baskets.Researchers in the area of memory and learning have found that \"chunking\" numerical information in three or four-digit groups makes the numbers easier to remember than a single sequence of seven or ten digits..\nIn the pages that follow, you will learn about the psychology of eating.  You will be given Case Studies (summarized from Mindless Eating by Brian Wansink) and be asked to determine the manipulated and responding variables.  Bon appetit!his link opens in a new window/tab)",
