          "contentBody": "Practice with Percentiles\nA group of high school Biology students were asked to identify correctly 14 different kinds of perennial flowers.  Below is a chart of scores showing how well each student did on the test.\nMindy\nWes\nTed\nToni\nFred\nSally\nStacey\nRon\nAnn\n2/14\n3/14\n7/14\n5/14\n5/14\n12/14\n6/14\n3/14\n8/14\nQ: What is Ted's percent?\nA: Divide 7 by 14 then multiply the quotient by 100.\nTed's percent is\n50%\nQ: What is Ted's percentile?\nA: To begin, reorder the scores from lowest to highest.\nWes\nRon\nToni\nFred\nStacey\nAnn\nTed\nSam\nSally\n3/14\n3/14\n5/14\n5/14\n6/14\n6/14\n7/14\n11/14\n12/14\nTed ranks in eighth position (from the lowest mark) in the class of 10.  Because the number of percentiles in any given data set is 100, we must multiply 10 (the number of scores) by another factor to equal 100.  In this case, we multiply 10 by 10 to get 100.  This means we simply multiply Ted's rank of 8 by 10 to get his percentile.\nTed's percentile is\n80\nQ: What is Sam's percentile?\nA:  Sam's percentile is\n                                                                                                Sam's percentile is\n                                                                                                         90\nSam's percentile means that 10% of people scored higher on the test than he did and 90% of people scored lower on the test than he did.\nIn some studies, researchers want to know how close each individual score is to the middle of the distribution.  Using the mean as the measure of the middle of a distribution, researchers calculate the average squared difference of the scores from the mean.  The result of this calculation is called the variance.  The variance is used to determine the standard deviation.  The standard deviation is the square root of the variance.\nWow... that sounds complicated!\nDon't worry, you won't be required to perform these types of calculations in the final exam (or project).  However, be familiar with the terms mean, mode, median, range, variance, and standard deviation.\nA simple example of how to calculate variance and standard deviation involves a dart game.  Six friends each throw one dart at a dart board much like the one in the photo above.  Three of the six friends get a \"bulls-eye\" (dead centre) worth 10 points.  Of the other three friends, one lands his dart in the first ring closest the centre and earns 9 points.  The second friend lands her dart in the third ring closest the centre and earns 7 points.  The last friend lands his dart in the fifth ring closest to the centre and earns 5 points.\nQ: What is the standard deviation in the dart game?\nStep One:  Determine the arithmetic average or mean from the data set by adding all six values and dividing the sum by six.\nScore\n10\n10\n10\n9\n7\n5\n10 + 10 + 10 + 9 + 7 + 5 = 51\n51/6 =  8.5\nStep Two:  Calculate the difference between each score and the mean value, and then square that difference.\nScore\nDifference from Mean\nSquare of Difference\n10\n10 - 8.5 = 1.5\n1.52 = 2.25\n10\n10 - 8.5 = 1.5\n1.52 = 2.25\n10\n10 - 8.5 = 1.5\n1.52 = 2.25\n9\n9 - 8.5 = 0.5\n0.52  = 0.25\n7\n7 - 8.5 = -1.0\n-1.02 = 1.00\n5\n5 - 8.5 = -3.5\n-3.52 = 12.25\nStep Three:  Determine the arithmetic average or mean of all the squared differences by adding all six calculated values together and dividing the sum by six.  This mean is also known as the variance.\nSquare of Difference\n2.25\n2.25\n2.25\n0.25\n1.00\n12.25\n2.25 + 2.25 + 2.25 + 0.25 + 1.00 + 12.25 = 20.25\n20.25 / 6 =  3.375\nStep Four: Determine the standard deviation by finding the square root of the variance.\n√3.375\nA: 1.837\nIf the value of standard deviation is small, the numbers in a data set are clustered closely together.  If a standard deviation is large, then the numbers are more spread out.",
