          "moduleSequence": 3,
          "moduleVisibilityLabel": "visible",
          "sourceHref": "сontent/ic19fa67b-e5b2-4b56-b3ab-03486e57abf0/Data Tables.html",
          "resourceKind": "html",
          "contentBody": "Basic Page\nData Tables\nWe're sitting at a table, does that count?\nCreating Tables to Support Conclusions\nIn social science research, analytical tables are designed to support conclusions about the relationship between variables.  Frequently, brief descriptions are presented with tables.  Some individuals prefer to read descriptions and barely glance at tables; others prefer to review well-designed tables and ignore the accompanying descriptions.  Because of this, both the table and its descriptive text must be carefully crafted.\nTable Description\nThe titles, headings, and footnotes in tables must convey clearly the general purpose of the table and explain the scaling and coding (if needed).  An effective table allows the reader to draw meaningful conclusions about the relationships among variables.\nTable Creation\nTables can be created using paper and pencil technology or they can be created using spreadsheet software.  Regardless of how a table is created, effective tables must have:\n✔️ a title that identifies the variables  (The title is usually in bold type.)\n✔️ correctly labelled columns and rows  (When appropriate, the left column or top row should contain the manipulated variable.  The bottom row or right column should contain the responding variable).\n✔️  an \"end\" right-hand column with the average for each trial, if  applicable units displayed in row or column headings only\n✔️ missing values displayed as a dash ( - ) and zeros as 0  (Tables should have no blank spaces).\n✔️ numbers aligned appropriately and to the same decimal place, if applicable\nTable Example 1: Eye-blink rates during various activities\nManipulated variable:  type of activity\nResponding variable:  blink rate\nControlled variables:  same time of day, same humidity level, same light level, same level of alertness in test subjects, same gender of test subjects\nBlinks per Minute\nTrial 1\nTrial 2\nTrial 3\nAverage\n8\n9\n7\n8\n7\n8\n6\n7\n12\n10\n11\n11\n22\n18\n20\n20\nAs you can see, the above table has a title (in bold), is properly labelled, and has a column for averages.  This is a well-designed table.\nTable Example 2: TV watching behaviour of pre-teens\nTables are best presented when the data is arranged or sorted appropriately.  For example, a table displaying the TV watching behaviour of 10-year-old boys in 11 countries could be sorted in order from highest percentage to lowest percentage.  The table could also be sorted alphabetically by country.  Compare the two methods below.  Which sorting method is the better?\nSorting by Country\nPercent of 10-year-old boys who watch more than five hours of television per day\n   14.8\n     6.0\n     6.1\n     5.5\n     4.3\n 11.9\n     9.1\n   12.7\n   17.5\n     4.7\n 21.6\nSorting by Percentages\nPercent of 10-year-old boys who watch more than five hours of television per day\n21.6\n17.5\n14.8\n12.7\n11.9\n  9.1\n  6.1\n  6.0\n  5.5\n  4.7\n  4.3\nData should be sorted by the most meaningful variable.  In the above examples, the alphabet is not the most meaningful variable.  In fact, name directories are really the only tables that need be sorted alphabetically.  The most meaningful variable is percentage of TV watching over 5 hours per week.\n(this link opens in a new window/tab)",
