          "sourceHref": "сontent/i0790696c-7e1e-4243-bab5-55f9ffd76145/Brain Damage Behaviour (1).pdf",
          "resourceKind": "pdf",
          "contentBody": "1\nCase Study 13\nBrain Damage and Ethical Behaviour\n\nDo we have a choice regarding how to behave or are we products of our genetic coding?\nThe nature-nurture debate is classic in the field of psychology.  Many people believe\nbehaviour results from a blend of genetic coding (nature), and familial upbringing (how we\nwere raised as children) -- nurture.  A study published in the journal Nature suggests that\nethical behaviour may be, at least in part, hardwired in the brain.\n\nThe Dilemma\nAmerican researchers presented a controversial scenario to 30 test subjects.  The\nparticipants were told that if they do harm to one person, they could prevent future harm to\nmany others.  For example, if you knew that someone with a deadly infectious disease was\nplanning to infect others (some of whom would die), would you kill the person or would\nyou let the person intentionally infect others?\n\nThe Results\n\nMany of the participants struggled in making a decision and\nmost said they would not kill the infected individual.  Six\nparticipants, however, stated a willingness to harm one\nindividual to protect others.  These six participants had\ndamage to a part of their brains called the ventromedial\nprefrontal cortex (VMPC).  The VMPC is part of the frontal\nlobe and the frontal lobe is linked to higher cognitive\nfunctioning.\n\n2\nRalph Adolphs, professor of psychology and neuroscience at the California Institute of\nTechnology, believes that VMPC brain damage results in abnormal social emotions.  The\nsix participants, he believed, lacked empathy and compassion.  Of the remaining 24\nparticipants, 12 had no brain damage and 12 had brain damage in areas other than the\nVMPC.\n\nAlthough correlation is not causation, this study implies that aversion to hurting others\nmay be hard-wired in the frontal lobe of the brain.  This leaves researchers with an\nimportant question:  Are social emotions necessary for moral judgments?  The research\ncontinues!",
