          "resourceKind": "pdf",
          "contentBody": "Case Study 14\nPavlov’s Conditioned Response Experiments\n\nRussian scientist Ivan Petrovich Pavlov was born in 1849.  Although he won a Nobel\nPrize for his studies of the digestive system, Pavlov is often best known for his “drooling\ndog” experiments.  When a dog encounters food, saliva starts to pour from the salivary\nglands located in the back of the oral cavity.  Saliva is needed to make food easier to\nswallow and it also contains enzymes that break down certain compounds such as starch.\n\nPavlov became interested in studying reflexes when he noticed that the dogs in his lab\ndrooled in the absence of food -- the logical stimulus for drooling.  Pavlov discovered the\ndogs were reacting to lab coats.  Each time the dogs were fed, the person who served the\nfood was wearing a lab coat.  In essence, the dogs reacted (drooled) whenever they saw a\nlab coat because they associated the coat with being fed.\n\nReflexes\nA reflex is a response to a stimulus.  For example, when light strikes our eyes, our pupils\nshrink or contract.  In situations where there is little light, our pupils expand or dilate.\nThis type of reflex or response is unconditioned.  Unconditioned reflexes are natural or\nbuilt-in, and the body responds in the same fashion every time the stimuli (the light for\nexample), is changed.  Drooling by dogs in the presence of food is an unconditioned\nreflex or response.  In a series of experiments, Pavlov tried to determine how drooling\ncould be conditioned by unnatural stimuli (such as lab coats).\n\nIn one of his experiments Pavlov struck a\nbell when the dogs were fed.  If the bell\nwas sounded in close association with their\nmeals, the dogs learned to associate the\nsound of the bell with food.  After repeated\ntrials of the bell and food being presented\ntogether, the dogs would salivate when the\nbell was sounded in the absence of food.\nThese experiments were first conducted in\nthe 1890s and 1900s.\n\nThe Results\nPavlov's discovered that environmental events (bell) that previously had no relation to a\ngiven reflex (drooling) could, through experience, trigger the reflex (drooling).  This\nform of learned response is called a conditioned reflex.  The process whereby dogs (or\nother animals) learn to connect a stimulus to a reflex is called conditioning.  Food\naversion is an example of a natural conditioned reflex.  For example, if an animal eats\nsomething with a distinctive taste and then experiences nausea and vomits, the animal\nwill not be eager to eat food with the same distinctive taste.  Linking nausea to taste is an\nevolutionarily successful strategy.\n\nAs a result of his experiments, Pavlov proved that animals (including humans) could be\ntrained to respond in a certain way to a particular stimulus.  An equally important\nprinciple in conditioned learning is that an established conditioned response (salivating in\nthe case of the dogs) decreases in intensity if the conditioned stimulus (bell) is repeatedly\npresented without the unconditioned stimulus (food).  This process is called extinction.\n\nConditioning forms the basis for much learned human behaviour.  Commercial\nadvertising, for example, effectively associates a product’s name (stimuli) to a given\nresponse (positive emotion).  In one taste test, elementary school children where given\ntwo identical hamburgers.  One hamburger was in a plain wrapper and one was in a\nMcDonald’s wrapper.  The vast majority of children indicated that the burger in the\nMcDonald’s wrapper tasted better.  The response in this case (satisfaction) may have\nbeen influenced by the McDonald’s wrapper (stimuli) although the burgers were\nidentical.  Do you think fast food companies want to condition this type of response?",
