          "contentBody": "Case Study 16\nBaby Albert\n\nBuilding on the research of Pavlov and his principles of conditioned reflexes, John\nWatson, an American Psychologist, and his research assistant, Rosalie Rayner, conducted\nan experiment whereby a fear response was conditioned in an otherwise healthy and\nfearless baby boy.  What Watson and Rayner wanted to prove was that behaviour is the\nresult of learning and that learning is a classically conditioned response to environmental\nstimulus.\n\nIn 1920, Watson and Rayner performed their now infamous experiment on a healthy baby\nboy named Albert.  Upon evaluation, they found Albert to be unusually calm and well-\nbehaved.  Nothing seemed to disturb or frighten the baby, not even fire.  The infant was\nconfronted suddenly and in rapid succession with a white rat, a rabbit, a dog, and a\nmonkey and with masks with and without hair, cotton wool, and burning newspapers.\nAlthough Albert had not been exposed to any of these stimuli before, at no time did\nAlbert display fear or apprehension.  To determine whether a fear reaction could be\nelicited in Albert, an extremely loud sound was made by striking a hammer on a steel bar\nbehind Albert’s head.  Indeed, Albert did not like this sound – he startled and his lips\nbegan to pucker and tremble.  On the third striking of the bar, Albert began crying.  This\nwas the first time a situation in the laboratory had produced any fear or crying in Albert.\nAt this time Albert was 9 months old.\n\nTwo months after their initial encounter with Albert, Watson and Rayner attempted to\ncondition Albert to fear a white rat.  Rayner supervised the baby while Albert interacted\nwith the rat.  Watson stood behind Albert and every time Albert reached for the rat,\nWatson smacked the bar with the hammer.  Just as predicted, the noise scared Albert.\n\nWatson and Rayner, over several days, paired the white rat with the\nloud noise.  As predicted, Albert eventually exhibited fear at the\npresence of the rat alone.  This reaction was similar to Pavolv’s\ndog’s salivation at the sound of a bell in the absence of food.\nWatson and Rayner had successfully conditioned, in Albert, fear at\nthe sight of a white rat.  Generalization had also occurred with\nAlbert as his fear was not confined to only white rats.  Albert\nbecame fearful of other furry animals, Watson's hair, sealskin coats\n-- even Santa Claus beards.\n\nUnfortunately for Albert, the conditioning was not reversed because Albert was adopted\nsoon after the conditioning occurred and Watson and Rayner were unable to locate him.",
